00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:44.273
without putting in the work,
that's all, it's a very simple.

00:10:44.273 --> 00:10:47.050
You can't get muscles without working out,

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.210
and you can't get success without working.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:51.210
And there's a lot of people
that push back at me,

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.760
especially in Europe and
more liberal points of view

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.240
around too much work it's
'cause they're not listening to

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:57.611
what's coming out of my mouth.

00:10:57.611 --> 00:11:01.390
My words are very simple,
as long as your work ethic

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:04.190
matches your ambition, you are very happy.

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:07.160
Meaning if you want to have
a nice work life balance

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.830
and have three days off
a week and be healthy,

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.700
and what have you, and recognize
that you're not going to

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.770
have mansions or reputation
or books or, great.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.010
I mean, I think that's, to be very frank,

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:21.700
there's a lot of times I
wish that was my ambition.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:24.150
To me the only stress in the system,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:27.170
is when people's ambition, AKA their mouth

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:28.930
doesn't match their actions.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:32.660
There's a lot of people
in on Weibo and Instagram,

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:35.210
and YouTube saying that they're
going to be billionaires,

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:36.290
they're going to rule the world,

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.535
who go to 45 music festivals a year,

00:11:38.535 --> 00:11:40.140
(all laugh)

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.690
and that is not going to happen.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:46.220
So to me, it's just matching
your ambition to your actions.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.290
Otherwise, I think it's a great thing

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.850
that people are optimistic
because I think optimism matters.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:53.650
- Okay, Malik, what did you want to say?

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.530
- Yeah, to elaborate about
the Buddhist lifestyle,

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:58.320
so it's also the young people nowadays.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.360
They think that they're
facing more challenges in like

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:03.990
a more competitive environment
than their parents,

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:05.990
because they have to
learn much more stuff,

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.130
they have to have much more skills,

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.477
going to college doesn't
guarantee anything at all.

00:12:10.477 --> 00:12:12.150
- [Gary] They're right, they're right.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:13.900
- So--
- [Gary] But the problem is

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.090
they're thinking about it
through their parents' eyes.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.400
- Yeah, and also like with almost,

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:20.060
like this much of competition.
- Do you understand though?

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:20.893
- Yeah.

00:12:20.893 --> 00:12:25.580
- Like college, to your point,
especially in mainland China

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.680
and high net worth into it,

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:31.480
the path of college then
sets you up is over.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:32.430
- [Malik] Yeah.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:33.320
- It's over.

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:34.230
- [Malik] It doesn't
guarantee you anything.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.160
- It's just over, like,
look, if you want to work

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:40.300
at Bain and McKinsey and Goldman
Sachs, big time education,

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.500
you can still have that
path, that's great.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:43.860
- [Mallik] I agree.
- Nothing wrong with that.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:48.530
The problem is the default
that a college education,

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.280
the pedals were off the rows.

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.883
Like it's outside of the
elite top 10 schools,

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:58.860
I think the schools that
are most dangerous are

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.750
schools 25 to 100, because
they had some brand

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.640
but they don't translate.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.818
They don't translate in
today's reality anymore.

00:13:06.818 --> 00:13:11.670
It just doesn't matter,
and for a parent at home,

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:12.503
especially in mainland China,

00:13:12.503 --> 00:13:14.700
this is back to what I jumped on,

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.865
I am very upset that you have
one child, to your point.

00:13:18.865 --> 00:13:21.290
And I don't know every detail,
but I'm aware enough to know

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:22.654
a lot of people are single children.

00:13:22.654 --> 00:13:24.862
(Malik laughs)

00:13:24.862 --> 00:13:29.810
The thing that I see
common in Southeast culture

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:31.570
and especially in mainland China,

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.080
is that for a lot of parents,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.476
their kid is a representation of them,

00:13:35.476 --> 00:13:37.090
and that's where it breaks.

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:37.923
- [Malik] Exactly.

00:13:37.923 --> 00:13:39.240
- And that's where it breaks in Israel,

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.330
and in breaks in Belarus,
and breaks in America.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.920
It breaks if a parent thinks
that a kid's accomplishment

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.050
is a reflection on them, because
now you have a disconnect.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:50.390
Now you're not a parent and a child,

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:52.133
you're a person with a product.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:56.520
And I think that that is scary to me.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:58.420
- True.
- Because going to Harvard

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.665
or Stanford or Yale is a badge
that people back in Beijing

00:14:02.665 --> 00:14:06.130
walk around proudly to
their contemporaries,

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:08.616
and they're fucking their kids up.

00:14:08.616 --> 00:14:10.900
- That's a word though
to use, that's so sure.

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:12.270
- That's it, that's the breakdown.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.090
So, but the kids are right.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:16.737
Here's what I would say
to the kids, "Too bad."

00:14:16.737 --> 00:14:18.294
(Malik laughs)

00:14:18.294 --> 00:14:20.630
I mean too bad that it's hard now.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:23.453
Like you have a lot of
things going for you too.

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:25.990
I promise you right now,

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.426
all of you would not switch
with your grandparents.

00:14:28.426 --> 00:14:29.384
- [Malik] Yeah.

00:14:29.384 --> 00:14:31.660
- If you're in China and
you're listening right now,

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:35.670
and you have the option of
having not college set you up

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:37.890
so easily, or be your grandparents

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:40.770
and live in that communist
China, I have a funny feeling

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:42.200
you're going to choose this.
(Malik laughs)

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.230
So to me, I have empathy
that it's different

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:47.728
and I've empathy that you've
been told education, education,

00:14:47.728 --> 00:14:50.322
education, college, college,
college, good college,

00:14:50.322 --> 00:14:53.480
get straight A's, straight
A's, alpha, alpha dragon, drag,

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:58.250
I understand, it's changed
but you're not going to switch

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.603
with your grandparents, so
what are you crying about?

00:15:01.603 --> 00:15:03.753
- [Raz] True.
- Life's about alternatives.

00:15:04.845 --> 00:15:06.070
- [Raz] That's true.
- It's less about alternative.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:09.480
So what do you want, you
want to, what do you want?

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:13.520
And I think most people
realize how good they have it.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:16.691
And so yes, it's changed,
yes, there's new difficulties,

00:15:16.691 --> 00:15:20.830
but it's so much better
than it used to be,

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:25.417
and so I can't get upset for
anybody who's living through,

00:15:25.417 --> 00:15:28.560
"Oh, darn it, my Yale
education is not going to

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.920
take care of me," especially
when a far bigger percentage

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:33.540
of kids from mainland China that are going

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:36.070
to these universities are
having it paid for by their

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:40.133
parents than the alternative
of a lot of Americans, so.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.327
- One thing is in Mainland
China, that is an optimism.

00:15:44.327 --> 00:15:46.820
It's a very optimistic point of view is,

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:50.090
the government is now
encouraging graduates

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.310
to take entrepreneurship
as their first job.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:54.460
And we see in Beijing
especially (mumbles).

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:58.650
Graduates who used to
go to the government of

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:01.350
the big companies to follow
what their parents think,

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.771
they open their own startups,
they're doing start ups.

00:16:03.771 --> 00:16:05.930
The parents have nothing to say about it

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:07.730
because the government endorsed it.

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:08.960
So it's kind of--

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:12.800
- I think that's great, look, America wins

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.430
because of entrepreneurship.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:17.050
It's offense, you're either
an offense or defense.

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:19.800
Europe's been on defense for 50 years,

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.550
that's why Europe sucks
on the global stage

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.290
of competitive business, I love Europe.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:27.460
I love Europe for one,
I was born in Europe.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:30.260
I love Europe for culture,
I like the kindness

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.150
and the empathy, I love
Europe for a lot of

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.680
its social points of view,
except when it goes too far

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.241
to the left, I love Europe,
but Europe can't win.

00:16:38.241 --> 00:16:43.241
Europe's soft, and so I think
it's great that China pushes.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.890
And look, I'm an American who
was born in the Soviet Union,

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:49.850
who absolutely believes
that China has one already,

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:53.230
meaning that when I'm an
old man or when I'm gone,

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:55.550
and my grandkids are like,
"China is the superpower."

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.970
I really believe that, because
then China runs its country

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:03.519
more like a business, meaning
it's a business, like me.

00:17:03.519 --> 00:17:07.000
I give freedom to my team so much,

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:08.920
except on a couple
things that matter to me,

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:13.136
and then it's a dictatorship,
that's more China, right?

00:17:13.136 --> 00:17:15.220
So I think China's model in a lot of ways,

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.340
I mean, socially forget.

00:17:16.340 --> 00:17:18.580
I'm talking about
entrepreneurship and business

00:17:18.580 --> 00:17:21.833
and the alpha stuff, I think
China's got a long way to go.

00:17:21.833 --> 00:17:25.520
There's soft skills and equal
rights and a million things

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:28.700
that make like, but I
think it'll get there

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:30.013
out of its leadership.

00:17:30.955 --> 00:17:35.650
So I think it's sitting very
pretty, I think it's great that

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:37.250
it's pushing kids to entrepreneurship.

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:41.610
My problem is nine out of 10
of these Chinese kids that are

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.640
22 year olds that are
jumping into entrepreneurship

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.020
have no chance because
they're not entrepreneurs.

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.920
- Yes, but that's good
because it's education.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:50.610
- Yes, but you have to be careful

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:54.940
because the one thing I fear
is when these kids fail,

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:58.840
and almost all of them will,
it's a very big scarlet letter

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:01.370
in that culture, and I
think psychologically,

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:04.720
you're putting a 23 year old
in a very vulnerable spot

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:08.030
where now they have a loss
publicly, there's no hiding.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:10.860
When you lose in school like
I did, you can blame people.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:13.470
When Iris loses at
VaynerMedia, she can blame me.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:16.250
When you work at a company or
when the government's involved

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:18.520
or when at school, you
can blame somebody else.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:21.030
When you have a business, it's your fault.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:23.650
And the only thing I would say,

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:25.960
it's already something that
concerns me in America,

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:30.529
where it is not a scarlet
letter to lose in business,

00:18:30.529 --> 00:18:33.780
but still, there's an enormous amount of,

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:35.920
because there's so many
fake entrepreneurs now

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.240
because everybody's pushing
it, and now you have students

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.430
who, I like when I lose, I
like when you guys look at me

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:43.790
and say, "He lost, he did it wrong,"

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:45.150
cause I'm an entrepreneur.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.410
But when you're a
student and you get an F,

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:51.140
it's devastating, so I
would caution one thing

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:54.010
for anybody who's
listening in mainland China

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:56.360
who thinks entrepreneurship
is such a freedom.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:00.360
When you lose, you have to
make sure that you're mentally

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.230
stable enough to be able
to take all that cynicism

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:06.150
and the critiques from everybody,
including your friends,

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:09.270
your friends' parents, your
parents, because if you don't,

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:12.790
you start going down a very
dangerous path that leads to

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:14.070
a very scary place.

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:17.950
We do not reconcile why
we have suicide in America

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:20.630
at the teenage level, it's
because modern parenting

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:24.080
is over coddling kids to an extreme level

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:26.730
where kids don't know how
to deal with adversity.

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:29.730
If you've been a great student
in the mainland China machine

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.620
and you now pop out and you start the Uber

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:37.004
or Tencent of dry cleaning and you fail,

00:19:37.004 --> 00:19:40.400
I don't want kids to go
into depression and suicide.

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.630
I think self-awareness
matters much more than

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:44.800
being an entrepreneur, for you and I,

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:47.210
the learning makes sense, it's our DNA.

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:49.090
I look at every loss as learning.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:51.320
That is not how people are wired,

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:54.990
so we have to be thoughtful
of, we go too far, right?

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.798
It's all college, it's
all entrepreneurship.

00:19:57.798 --> 00:20:00.620
We need to find the right
balance of understanding.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:04.240
We need to do a much better
job, zero to 18, to understand

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:08.220
somebody's makeup, their
emotional makeup, their skillset.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:10.340
The way we do school today,
and the way we structure

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:13.040
our kids for success in the first 18 years

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:15.210
is so broken worldwide.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.100
And so for me, I'm thinking
a lot about the next 20,

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.630
30 years of my life, how
can I impact that least,

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:22.450
to now I impact by putting out content

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:24.576
and getting the 500 DMS I
get every day of, "Thank you,

00:20:24.576 --> 00:20:28.310
thank you, thank you, you're
helping me, you're a shield."

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:31.920
That's my first step, but
over time, I'd like to do more

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.800
because it is the most important thing.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.784
Because if your parented well,

00:20:35.784 --> 00:20:38.650
you can see it from a mile
away and when you're not,

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:39.910
you can see it from a mile away.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:44.400
And I think too many parents
view their kids as a product.

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:46.893
- Yeah, I have an idea for you may use.

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:50.940
You were said that, right,
it's about what is your target.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:53.110
If your target is to
win, to go all the way,

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:54.390
you put everything in.

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:56.990
I think we're going to era where there is

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:59.700
like micro innovation,
micro-entrepreneurship.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:01.110
You don't set your goal to be the next--

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:03.802
- I agree, I agree.
- It's a life lifestyle issue.

00:21:03.802 --> 00:21:05.394
I want to be in my lifestyle, okay.

00:21:05.394 --> 00:21:06.500
- I agree.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:09.800
- It's going to be split into
three; machines, lifestyle

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:11.270
and innovation style.
- I agree.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:14.005
I agree so much, I push it very heavily.

00:21:14.005 --> 00:21:17.650
What's wrong with having a
business doing 137,000 a year

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:19.630
and you're very happy selling donuts?

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:20.463
- [Paps] Yes.

00:21:20.463 --> 00:21:21.490
- I want it very badly.

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:23.920
The problem is venture capital

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:26.980
and the modern technology media,
everything's about unicorns

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:28.476
and billion dollars.
- Crowdfunding.

00:21:28.476 --> 00:21:30.039
Crowdfunding makes it easier,

00:21:30.039 --> 00:21:31.460
don't need the venture capitalist.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:34.100
- You're preaching,
how about a better one?

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:35.350
How about just build a fucking business

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:37.180
and work off your own cashflow?

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:38.895
- [Paps] Good education.
(Gary laughs)

00:21:38.895 --> 00:21:41.550
Actually, this is what
we do in some cases.

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:44.820
- I mean, Jesus Christ,
I mean like why not just,

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.827
I mean, VaynerMedia is
a $200 billion company.

00:21:46.827 --> 00:21:48.580
I raised zero capital,

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:50.830
I sold somebody a $60,000 consulting gig.

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:52.570
I was in a different place in my career,

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:53.710
but I still could have raised it.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.960
Like learning how to make
money is the number one skill.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.930
Sell something, Singles Day
sells trillions of dollars

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:01.551
of shit every year, figure it out,

00:22:01.551 --> 00:22:05.493
get your $100 out of it, Jesus.

00:22:05.493 --> 00:22:08.050
And I mean this, I want to
teach kids more to learn

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:10.820
how to make money versus raising money,

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:12.320
because that's a skill
that never goes away.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:14.301
You can always raise money later.

00:22:14.301 --> 00:22:17.390
And he'll tell you it's a
lot easier to raise money

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:19.330
